<section class="section section-faq">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">05 · Fair questions</p>
    <h2>Asked constantly, answered honestly.</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>How do I know if my dog's food is the problem?</summary>
      <p>The most common tells are persistent itching without fleas, heavy shedding, recurring ear infections, gas, loose or inconsistent stools, and low energy. None of these prove it's the food — but food is the cheapest, safest first variable to change, and coat and stool usually respond within 4–6 weeks of a genuine upgrade.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is expensive dog food actually better?</summary>
      <p>Price alone proves nothing — some boutique brands charge a premium for the same formulation quality as the grocery aisle. What matters is who formulated it, whether named proteins lead the ingredient panel, and whether the recipe matches your dog rather than an average dog. Our guides show you how to check all three on any label.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do supplements really do anything?</summary>
      <p>The honest answer: the right ones, for the right dog, yes — joint support for aging large breeds and omega-3s for skin and coat have the strongest track records. Sprinkling random powders on bad food does nothing. Fix the bowl first, then supplement the specific issue that remains.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Why do you recommend one storefront?</summary>
      <p>Because we've used it for years, it's vet-formulated, and it builds the plan around your dog instead of selling you a bag. Full transparency: the author is an independent consultant for that company and earns a commission when you buy there — it's how this site stays free. We recommend nothing we don't feed our own dogs.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Should I talk to my vet before switching food?</summary>
      <p>Yes — especially if your dog has a diagnosed condition, is on medication, or is a puppy or senior. Nothing on this site is veterinary advice. A good vet will welcome the question, and the guides here will make that conversation more useful.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

